## Final Judgement
The fact-check evidence consistently shows that as of September 2020, the US federal government had no announced plans, laws, or proposals to mandate COVID-19 vaccination for all residents. Multiple sources indicate the strategy was focused on voluntary uptake, phased allocation based on risk, and addressing hesitancy, with experts explicitly stating that universal mandates were not under consideration due to legal, logistical, and public trust barriers.

No information is lacking; the evidence is clear and comprehensive.

Based on the recorded evidence, the US federal government's documented plans in 2020 emphasized voluntary vaccination and did not include any intention to force a COVID-19 vaccine on everyone, thereby refuting the claim. `refuted`

### Verdict: REFUTED

### Justification
As of September 2020, the U.S. federal government had no announced plans, laws, or proposals to mandate COVID-19 vaccination for all residents. Multiple sources, including a [Harvard Law analysis](https://blog.petrieflom.law.harvard.edu/2020/09/15/covid19-vaccine-mandate-compulsory/), a [Congressional Research Service report](https://sgp.fas.org/crs/misc/RS21414.pdf), and the [administration's own distribution strategy](https://www.defense.gov/News/Releases/Release/Article/2349357/trump-administration-releases-covid-19-vaccine-distribution-strategy/), indicate the public strategy focused on voluntary uptake, phased allocation prioritizing specific groups, and addressing hesitancy, explicitly rejecting the feasibility and appropriateness of a universal federal mandate at that time. Therefore, the claim is refuted.